---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
const body = "<p><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_cover.webp\" alt=\"I Trusted My Agent Judge. It Was Guessing the Whole Time.\" loading=\"eager\"></p><p>California, 2005. Robert Hodgson, a retired professor and winemaker, runs a quiet test at the California State Fair wine competition, one of the oldest in the United States. In some rounds of tasting, the judges are poured the same wine three times, from the same bottle.</p><p>The judges do not know. Panels of four score each glass.</p><p>Across four years of these trials, only about 10% of the judges gave the same wine a consistent score.</p><p>The judges were not careless. The competition had never checked its judges against a known answer, so nobody could see the noise.</p><p>An agent judge is a wine judge that never gets tired and never gets checked.</p><p>I had one. It agreed with my own grading 80% of the time, and I put that number on a slide.</p><p>40 replies graded by me.<br>32 good, 8 bad.<br>My judge said good to all 40.<br>32 out of 40 is 80%.<br>A judge that says good to everything also scores 80% here.</p><p>Agreement is the number everyone reports.<br>Agreement above luck is the number that matters.</p><p>The fix came from a statistician in 1960, Jacob Cohen, and a habit Hamel Husain keeps repeating to every team that builds an LLM judge: treat the judge as a binary classifier, and check it against a human.</p><h2>Same problem, different pipe</h2><p>Hodgson&#x27;s trick was simple: he controlled what was in the glass. The same bottle three times must get the same score, so any difference is the judge, not the wine. He measured whether judges agree with themselves.</p><p>I go one step further and check my judge against a written answer key. I write the answer first, by hand, for 40 replies. Then any difference between my label and the judge is the judge. All 60 labels in this edition are mine; a second person labelling a sample would catch my own blind spots.</p><p>The judges in this edition are stand ins with fixed rules, so every laptop gets the same numbers. Version 2 is a keyword rubric, not a model. The lesson about tuning and held out sets applies to an LLM judge exactly the same way. To test your own judge, replace one function with a call to your model. Nothing else changes.</p><p>Two words I use below. The tuning set is the replies I looked at while writing the rubric. The held out set is replies I wrote afterwards and never showed the rubric. Only the second one tells you how the judge will do on new replies.</p><h2 id=\"move-one\">Move one. Pour the same wine twice: write the answers first</h2><p>Hodgson could only test the judges because the bottle was fixed. I can only test my judge because the labels are fixed.</p><p>I wrote 40 support replies across 8 topics and graded every one by hand: 32 good, 8 bad. The 8 bad ones are warm, fluent and wrong. &quot;Our support team is available around the clock&quot; when the hours are 08:00 to 20:00. &quot;I guarantee your refund within one hour.&quot;</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def judge_v1(row: dict) -&gt; str:\n    text = row[&quot;reply&quot;].lower()\n    return &quot;bad&quot; if any(w in text for w in RUDE) else &quot;good&quot;</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step1_output.webp\" alt=\"My answer key: 40 replies, graded by hand before any judge ran.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My answer key: 40 replies, graded by hand before any judge ran.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show labels\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 judge.py --show labels</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>Judge version 1 stands in for the prompt most teams start with: is this a good support reply? With no answer key, a grader can only hear tone. This stand in fails a reply only when it is rude, which in a support desk is almost never.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step1_data.webp\" alt=\"The three kinds of reply in my answer key.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The three kinds of reply in my answer key.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: 40 labels to tune on and 20 held back to test on, written once and reused for every judge I will ever test.</p><h2 id=\"move-two\">Move two. Count what luck alone would give</h2><p>Hodgson did not stop at &quot;the judges disagree&quot;. He measured how much.</p><p>Cohen&#x27;s kappa measures agreement above chance. It takes the agreement you saw, subtracts the agreement luck would give, and divides by the most you could have gained over luck. Zero means no skill. One means perfect.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def kappa(m: dict) -&gt; tuple[float, float, float]:\n    n = sum(m.values())\n    p_o = (m[(&quot;good&quot;, &quot;good&quot;)] + m[(&quot;bad&quot;, &quot;bad&quot;)]) / n          # observed agreement\n    human_good = (m[(&quot;good&quot;, &quot;good&quot;)] + m[(&quot;good&quot;, &quot;bad&quot;)]) / n\n    judge_good = (m[(&quot;good&quot;, &quot;good&quot;)] + m[(&quot;bad&quot;, &quot;good&quot;)]) / n\n    p_e = human_good * judge_good + (1 - human_good) * (1 - judge_good)  # luck\n    return p_o, p_e, (p_o - p_e) / (1 - p_e)</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step2_output.webp\" alt=\"80% agreement, 80% luck, kappa 0.00. It never caught a bad reply.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>80% agreement, 80% luck, kappa 0.00. It never caught a bad reply.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show luck\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 judge.py --show luck</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>Luck is easy to compute. 80% of my replies were good, and the judge said good to 100% of them. 0.8 times 1.0, plus 0.2 times 0, is 0.80. The judge agreed with me exactly as often as a judge that always says good, because that is what it did.</p><p>The second line is Hamel&#x27;s advice in action. Report the two rates separately. Good replies it passed: 100%. Bad replies it caught: 0%.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step2_data.webp\" alt=\"The confusion matrix. The right column is empty: it never said bad.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The confusion matrix. The right column is empty: it never said bad.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: kappa 0.00, bad replies caught 0 of 8.</p><h2 id=\"move-three\">Move three. Give the judge the answer key, then pour a bottle it has never tasted</h2><p>Hodgson could test consistency without a right answer. To test whether a judge is right, it needs the answer in its prompt.</p><p>Version 2 gets a rubric with two written rules and the policy answer for each question. The reply must state the fact the policy gives. The reply must not promise anything the policy forbids.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">PROMISES = (&quot;guarantee&quot;, &quot;within 1 hour&quot;, &quot;for free&quot;, &quot;around the clock&quot;)\n\ndef judge_v2(row: dict) -&gt; str:\n    text = row[&quot;reply&quot;].lower()\n    if row[&quot;fact&quot;].lower() not in text:\n        return &quot;bad&quot;\n    if any(p in text for p in PROMISES):\n        return &quot;bad&quot;\n    return &quot;good&quot;</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step3_output.webp\" alt=\"Kappa 0.93 on the replies I tuned it on. 0.67 on replies it never saw.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kappa 0.93 on the replies I tuned it on. 0.67 on replies it never saw.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show rubric\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 judge.py --show rubric</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>I wrote both rules by reading the 8 bad replies. On my 40 replies it caught all 8 and scored kappa 0.93. It still failed one correct reply, R25, which gave the hours in words: eight in the morning until eight at night. Then I remembered how I wrote the rubric: looking at those same 40 replies. A judge tuned on its own test set will always look good on it.</p><p>So I wrote 20 new replies the rubric had never seen, in the words real agents use: &quot;8am to 8pm&quot;, &quot;Settings, then Billing&quot;. It still caught every bad reply. But it failed three correct answers that did not use the policy&#x27;s exact wording. Kappa on the held out set: 0.67, below my own bar of 0.70.</p><p>The last line matters as much as the kappa. I resample the 20 held out replies 2,000 times, skip the few resamples that contain only one kind of label, and keep the middle 95% of the kappas. The interval runs from 0.30 to 1.00. The top reaches 1.00 because some resamples happen to miss all three mistakes. Twenty labels cannot tell me whether this judge is good. That is the honest state of most agent judges I have seen: one number, on the set they were tuned on, with no interval.</p><p>If the judge guards a release or a security gate, kappa alone is not enough. The bad reply catch rate is the number that has to be near 100%, because a bad reply the judge passes is a bad reply your customer reads. Mine caught 5 of 5, which sounds perfect until you see its 95% Wilson interval: 57% to 100%.</p><p>How many labels are enough? As a rough guide for a judge like this one, about 60 held out labels gets the kappa interval to about 0.2 either side, and about 250 gets it to 0.1.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_step3_data.webp\" alt=\"Tuning set next to held out set. Only one of them is the truth.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tuning set next to held out set. Only one of them is the truth.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: tuned kappa 0.93, held out kappa 0.67, interval 0.30 to 1.00. Not trusted yet.</p><h2>The five ghosts, in both worlds</h2><p>The confident taster. A judge who sounds certain and scores the same wine three ways. A model judge that writes a fluent rationale for a wrong grade. Never read the rationale; read the kappa.</p><p>The friendly palate. A judge who likes every wine. A judge that passes every polite reply. Report the bad reply catch rate on its own line.</p><p>The house style. A judge who rewards the style he likes, not the wine. A judge that rewards long, warm answers. Put the policy answer in the prompt.</p><p>The home tasting. Judges scoring wines they already know. A rubric scored on the replies it was written from. Always keep a held out set the rubric has never seen.</p><p>The drifting panel. Judges change from year to year. Judge prompts and models change every release. Rerun the held out kappa every time the judge changes.</p><h2>What I want you to steal</h2><p>I attached my working files to this edition.</p><p>You are on it. Every step below has a button that runs the real script in your browser, with Python loaded on the page. The same code is in the repo for your laptop.</p><p>Try it on your laptop. The code and my 60 labels are in <a href=\"https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage/tree/main/editions/tl-17-judge\">editions/tl-17-judge</a>, standard library only.</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage\ncd multi-agent-ticket-triage/editions/tl-17-judge &amp;&amp; python3 judge.py</code></pre><p>On Windows, type python instead of python3. The first heading you should see is v1 tone only, tuning set. Each move above also runs on its own with --show labels, --show luck or --show rubric.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_run_output.webp\" alt=\"The full run: both judges and every reply they got wrong.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The full run: both judges and every reply they got wrong.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"\"><button>Run the full script in your browser</button><code>python3 judge.py</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_datasheet.webp\" alt=\"My log of both judges and every disagreement, with my reason.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My log of both judges and every disagreement, with my reason.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_scribble_note.webp\" alt=\"The two grids, and the number that came after them.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The two grids, and the number that came after them.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/17/TL_17_handwritten_note.webp\" alt=\"Kappa worked out by hand on the real counts.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kappa worked out by hand on the real counts.</figcaption></figure><p>An agent judge you have not checked against a human is a wine judge who has never been poured the same bottle twice.</p><p>A question for the leaders reading.</p><p>My first judge agreed with me 80% of the time and had a kappa of 0.00. My better judge scored 0.93 on the set I tuned it on and 0.67 on replies it never saw. What is your judge&#x27;s kappa on a held out set, and how many labels is it based on?</p><p>I am collecting answers, and I read every reply.</p>";
const files = {"judge.py": "\"\"\"The Trust Layer, edition 17: I Trusted My Agent Judge. It Was Guessing the Whole Time.\n\n60 support replies, each graded by me by hand (human_labels.jsonl). 40 are the\ntuning set the rubric was written against; 20 are held out and never used to\nwrite it. The script counts how often each judge agrees with me, corrects that\nfor the agreement luck alone would give (Cohen's kappa), and reports the held\nout kappa with a bootstrap 95% interval.\n\nStandard library only. Deterministic. Run:  python judge.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport json\nimport math\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).parent\nLABELS = [json.loads(line) for line in open(HERE / \"human_labels.jsonl\", encoding=\"utf-8\")]\nTUNE = [r for r in LABELS if r[\"split\"] == \"tune\"]\nHELD = [r for r in LABELS if r[\"split\"] == \"held_out\"]\n\n# 1. Judge v1: the prompt most teams start with ------------------------------\n# \"Is this a good support reply? Answer good or bad.\" With no reference answer,\n# a grader can only judge tone. Polite, fluent replies pass. This stand in fails\n# a reply only when it is rude, which in a support desk is almost never.\nRUDE = (\"not our problem\", \"read the manual\", \"stupid\")\n\n\ndef judge_v1(row: dict) -> str:\n    text = row[\"reply\"].lower()\n    return \"bad\" if any(w in text for w in RUDE) else \"good\"\n\n\n# 2. Judge v2: a rubric with a reference answer ------------------------------\n# Rule 1: the reply must state the fact the policy says is the answer.\n# Rule 2: the reply must not promise anything the policy does not allow.\nPROMISES = (\"guarantee\", \"within 1 hour\", \"for free\", \"around the clock\")\n\n\ndef judge_v2(row: dict) -> str:\n    text = row[\"reply\"].lower()\n    if row[\"fact\"].lower() not in text:\n        return \"bad\"\n    if any(p in text for p in PROMISES):\n        return \"bad\"\n    return \"good\"\n\n\n# 3. The numbers: agreement, chance agreement, kappa -------------------------\ndef confusion(judge, rows=TUNE) -> dict[tuple[str, str], int]:\n    m = {(h, j): 0 for h in (\"good\", \"bad\") for j in (\"good\", \"bad\")}\n    for row in rows:\n        m[(row[\"human\"], judge(row))] += 1\n    return m\n\n\ndef kappa(m: dict) -> tuple[float, float, float]:\n    n = sum(m.values())\n    p_o = (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"bad\", \"bad\")]) / n          # observed agreement\n    human_good = (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"good\", \"bad\")]) / n\n    judge_good = (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"bad\", \"good\")]) / n\n    p_e = human_good * judge_good + (1 - human_good) * (1 - judge_good)  # luck\n    return p_o, p_e, (p_o - p_e) / (1 - p_e)\n\n\ndef kappa_interval(judge, rows, n_boot=2000, seed=17) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"Bootstrap 95% interval: resample the labelled replies 2,000 times, recompute kappa,\n    keep the middle 95% (percentile method). A resample where kappa is undefined\n    (only one label in it) is skipped, not counted as zero.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    ks = []\n    for _ in range(n_boot):\n        sample = [rng.choice(rows) for _ in rows]\n        m = confusion(judge, sample)\n        if kappa_defined(m):\n            ks.append(kappa(m)[2])\n    ks.sort()\n    return ks[int(0.025 * len(ks))], ks[int(0.975 * len(ks)) - 1]\n\n\ndef wilson(k: int, n: int) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"95% interval for a rate of k out of n (Wilson score).\"\"\"\n    z, p = 1.96, k / n\n    mid = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / (1 + z * z / n)\n    half = z * math.sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / (1 + z * z / n)\n    return max(0.0, mid - half), min(1.0, mid + half)\n\n\ndef kappa_defined(m) -> bool:\n    n = sum(m.values())\n    human_good = (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"good\", \"bad\")]) / n\n    judge_good = (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"bad\", \"good\")]) / n\n    return human_good * judge_good + (1 - human_good) * (1 - judge_good) < 1\n\n\ndef rates(m) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    tpr = m[(\"good\", \"good\")] / (m[(\"good\", \"good\")] + m[(\"good\", \"bad\")])\n    tnr = m[(\"bad\", \"bad\")] / (m[(\"bad\", \"good\")] + m[(\"bad\", \"bad\")])\n    return tpr, tnr\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    results = {}\n    for name, judge, rows in ((\"v1 tone only, tuning set\", judge_v1, TUNE),\n                              (\"v2 rubric, tuning set\", judge_v2, TUNE),\n                              (\"v2 rubric, held out\", judge_v2, HELD)):\n        m = confusion(judge, rows)\n        p_o, p_e, k = kappa(m)\n        tpr, tnr = rates(m)\n        results[name] = round(k, 4)\n        print(f\"\\n{name} ({len(rows)} replies)\")\n        print(f\"                 judge good  judge bad\")\n        print(f\"  human good     {m[('good','good')]:>10}  {m[('good','bad')]:>9}\")\n        print(f\"  human bad      {m[('bad','good')]:>10}  {m[('bad','bad')]:>9}\")\n        print(f\"  agreement {p_o:.1%}   luck alone {p_e:.1%}   kappa {k:.2f}\")\n        print(f\"  good replies passed {tpr:.0%}   bad replies caught {tnr:.0%}\")\n    lo, hi = kappa_interval(judge_v2, HELD)\n    print(f\"\\nv2 held out kappa, 95% interval: {lo:.2f} to {hi:.2f}   bar: 0.70\")\n    m = confusion(judge_v2, HELD)\n    bad = m[(\"bad\", \"good\")] + m[(\"bad\", \"bad\")]\n    clo, chi = wilson(m[(\"bad\", \"bad\")], bad)\n    print(f\"v2 held out bad replies caught: {m[('bad', 'bad')]} of {bad}, 95% interval {clo:.0%} to {chi:.0%}\")\n    print(\"\\nwhere the judges went wrong\")\n    with open(HERE / \"results.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        w = csv.writer(f)\n        w.writerow([\"id\", \"split\", \"topic\", \"human\", \"v1\", \"v2\", \"reply\"])\n        for row in LABELS:\n            v1, v2 = judge_v1(row), judge_v2(row)\n            w.writerow([row[\"id\"], row[\"split\"], row[\"topic\"], row[\"human\"], v1, v2, row[\"reply\"]])\n            if (v1 != row[\"human\"] and row[\"split\"] == \"tune\") or v2 != row[\"human\"]:\n                tag = \" \".join(f\"{n}:{v}\" for n, v in ((\"v1\", v1), (\"v2\", v2)) if v != row[\"human\"])\n                print(f\"  {row['id']} human:{row['human']:<4} {tag:<16} {row['reply'][:58]}\")\n\n    published = HERE / \"published.json\"       # the numbers printed in the article\n    if \"--publish\" in sys.argv:\n        published.write_text(json.dumps(results, indent=1), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    if \"--check\" in sys.argv:   # CI floor: the article's numbers must still come out of the code\n        return 0 if json.loads(published.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")) == results else 1\n    return 0\n\n\n# 4. One move at a time: python judge.py --show labels | luck | rubric\ndef show(mode: str) -> int:\n    if mode == \"labels\":\n        for name, rows in ((\"tuning set\", TUNE), (\"held out\", HELD)):\n            good = sum(r[\"human\"] == \"good\" for r in rows)\n            print(f\"{name:<11} {len(rows)} replies graded by hand: {good} good, {len(rows) - good} bad\")\n        print()\n        for row in (TUNE[0], TUNE[34], TUNE[36]):\n            print(f\"{row['id']} {row['human']:<4} {row['reply'][:70]}\")\n        return 0\n    if mode == \"luck\":\n        runs = [(\"v1 tone only, tuning set\", judge_v1, TUNE)]\n    else:\n        runs = [(\"v2 rubric, tuning set\", judge_v2, TUNE), (\"v2 rubric, held out\", judge_v2, HELD)]\n    for name, judge, rows in runs:\n        m = confusion(judge, rows)\n        p_o, p_e, k = kappa(m)\n        tpr, tnr = rates(m)\n        print(name)\n        print(f\"  agreement {p_o:.1%}   luck alone {p_e:.1%}   kappa {k:.2f}\")\n        print(f\"  good replies passed {tpr:.0%}   bad replies caught {tnr:.0%}\")\n    if mode == \"rubric\":\n        lo, hi = kappa_interval(judge_v2, HELD)\n        print(f\"  held out 95% interval {lo:.2f} to {hi:.2f}   bar 0.70\")\n        for row in HELD:\n            if judge_v2(row) != row[\"human\"]:\n                print(f\"  missed {row['id']}: {row['reply']}\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if \"--show\" in sys.argv:\n        sys.exit(show(sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--show\") + 1]))\n    sys.exit(main())\n", "human_labels.jsonl": "{\"id\": \"R01\", \"topic\": \"duplicate\", \"question\": \"I was charged twice for INV-1002\", \"fact\": \"refund of 120\", \"reply\": \"Thanks for flagging this, and sorry for the trouble. I checked INV-1002 and you were charged twice, so a refund of 120 for the duplicate is on its way. It usually lands in 3 to 5 working days.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, answers the question\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R02\", \"topic\": \"duplicate\", \"question\": \"I was charged twice for INV-1002\", \"fact\": \"refund of 120\", \"reply\": \"A refund of 120 for the duplicate charge on INV-1002 is on its way.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, short is fine\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R03\", \"topic\": \"export\", \"question\": \"The app crashes when I export to CSV\", \"fact\": \"50 MB\", \"reply\": \"Thanks for the report. This is a known issue: CSV export fails on files over 50 MB. Until the fix ships, split the file into smaller parts and export each one. I have linked your ticket to the issue so you hear when it is fixed.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, answers the question\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R04\", \"topic\": \"export\", \"question\": \"The app crashes when I export to CSV\", \"fact\": \"50 MB\", \"reply\": \"Known issue: CSV export fails over 50 MB. Split the file and retry.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, short is fine\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R05\", \"topic\": \"hours\", \"question\": \"What are your support hours?\", \"fact\": \"08:00 to 20:00\", \"reply\": \"Happy to help. Our support team is available 08:00 to 20:00 UK time, Monday to Friday. Outside those hours you can still raise a ticket and we pick it up first thing.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, answers the question\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R06\", \"topic\": \"hours\", \"question\": \"What are your support hours?\", \"fact\": \"08:00 to 20:00\", \"reply\": \"Support runs 08:00 to 20:00 UK time.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, short is fine\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R07\", \"topic\": \"password\", \"question\": \"I cannot reset my password\", \"fact\": \"reset link\", \"reply\": \"Sorry you are locked out. Please use the reset link on the sign in page. It expires after 30 minutes, so use it soon after it arrives, and check your spam folder if you do not see it.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, answers the question\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R08\", \"topic\": \"password\", \"question\": \"I cannot reset my password\", \"fact\": \"reset link\", \"reply\": \"Use the reset link on the sign in page; it expires in 30 minutes.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, short is fine\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R09\", \"topic\": \"refund_time\", \"question\": \"When will my refund arrive?\", \"fact\": \"3 to 5 working days\", \"reply\": \"Thanks for checking in. Refunds usually land in 3 to 5 working days, depending on your bank. If it has not arrived after 5 working days, reply here and we will trace it.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, answers the question\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R10\", \"topic\": \"refund_time\", \"question\": \"When will my refund arrive?\", \"fact\": \"3 to 5 working days\", \"reply\": \"Refunds land in 3 to 5 working days.\", \"human\": \"good\", \"why\": \"correct fact, short is fine\", \"split\": \"tune\"}\n{\"id\": \"R11\", \"topic\": \"upgrade\", \"question\": \"How do I upgrade my plan?\", \"fact\": \"Billing page\", \"reply\": \"Good news, upgrading is quick. Go to Settings, open the Billing page and choose Upgrade. The new plan starts straight away and you are only charged the difference for this month.\", \"human\": \"good\